# Base URL of the Kairos FastAPI backend.
# Server-only on purpose. Do NOT rename this to NEXT_PUBLIC_* — the browser
# must not hold the backend address or call it directly.
KAIROS_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000

# Which founder this dashboard reads, in local_shared mode ONLY.
#
# In supabase mode the founder comes from GET /me — the membership rows for
# the signed-in user's own token — and this value is ignored. It has to be:
# one variable naming one founder for the whole deployment means two people
# who sign in as themselves are shown the same inbox.
#
# The backend seeds `founder_demo` from data/demo_founder.json on startup.
KAIROS_FOUNDER_ID=founder_demo

# Timeout (ms) for calls to the backend. Every call this app makes is short:
# starting a run creates a job and returns immediately, and the dashboard
# polls for the result. The run's own ceiling is the backend's
# KAIROS_RUN_TIMEOUT_S, not a socket held open here.
KAIROS_API_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# Bearer token for the backend, when the backend sets KAIROS_API_TOKEN.
# Server-only: it is attached inside src/lib/api.ts and never reaches the
# browser. Permitted only when KAIROS_AUTH_MODE=local_shared. Leave empty
# when the backend runs open on localhost. Never set this in Vercel
# production — a missing Supabase session used to fall back to this token
# and act as the founder for whoever loaded the page.
KAIROS_API_TOKEN=

# Which identity story this dashboard tells. `local_shared` is a laptop.
# `supabase` requires NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL and NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY
# and never falls back to KAIROS_API_TOKEN. Vercel production and preview
# deploys always use supabase, even if this is left as local_shared.
KAIROS_AUTH_MODE=local_shared

# ── Sign-in (Supabase Auth) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Set both to turn on accounts. Leave both empty for the local single-founder
# mode, where there is no sign-in and the backend is reached with the shared
# KAIROS_API_TOKEN below. That mode is for a laptop: nothing is gated, and
# anyone who can load the page can trigger runs and read every draft.
#
# Both are NEXT_PUBLIC_ and that is correct here. The anon key is designed to
# ship to browsers — it is a public identifier carrying no privilege of its
# own, and every request it makes is still checked against the signed-in user.
#
# The service-role key is the one that must NEVER carry this prefix, or any
# prefix. It is not read anywhere in this app.
#
# The URL is a URL, not a key. The Supabase dashboard shows the project URL
# and the publishable key next to each other, and pasting the key into both
# slots is the easy mistake — it used to leave the dashboard silently running
# with no sign-in at all, showing the synthetic demo founder to anyone who
# loaded it. `supabaseUrlProblem()` in src/lib/supabase/config.ts now names
# that case on the login page instead.
#
#   Project Settings -> Data API -> Project URL
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

#   Project Settings -> API Keys -> publishable key (`sb_publishable_...`)
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ── Enabling Google and GitHub sign-in ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# Nothing below is a variable. These are the steps in the Supabase dashboard
# that make the two provider buttons on /login work; the app needs no further
# configuration once they are done.
#
#  1. Authentication -> Sign In / Providers -> enable Google and GitHub.
#
#  2. Each provider needs an OAuth app of its own, and each gives you a client
#     id and secret to paste into Supabase:
#       Google  — console.cloud.google.com -> APIs & Services -> Credentials
#                 -> Create OAuth client ID -> Web application.
#       GitHub  — github.com/settings/developers -> New OAuth App.
#
#  3. The provider's own "Authorised redirect URI" is Supabase's callback,
#     NOT this app's. Supabase shows the exact value on the provider page:
#       https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback
#
#  4. Authentication -> URL Configuration -> Redirect URLs. Add one line per
#     origin people sign in from. This IS this app's callback:
#       http://localhost:3000/auth/callback
#       https://<your-app>.vercel.app/auth/callback
#     Preview deploys get generated subdomains, so add a wildcard for them:
#       https://<your-app>-*.vercel.app/auth/callback
#     A missing entry here is the usual cause of "requested path is invalid"
#     after a provider consent screen.
#
# A first sign-in creates a founder automatically — see
# KAIROS_AUTO_PROVISION_FOUNDER in the root .env.example. Anyone who can
# complete one of these flows therefore gets a workspace, so restrict who may
# sign in at the provider before this is public.
